daemon: Keep a latched drag alive through fast motion, re-latch after a lift

Chris: click-and-drag broke more the faster he dragged. Causes found in the
pkgrel 12 latch, all only reachable at speed:

 - The contact tracker hands out a new index when a contact moves farther than
   0.15 (normalized) between frames, and the 5 mm LiftGraceMs match cannot
   bridge that either. The drag contact then vanished from the contact list
   (held for LiftGraceMs, then gone) and the latch ended the drag. The latch now
   follows the drag by the real contact nearest to its last position, within
   25 mm plus 3 times its last step (up to 60 mm), and ends only when no such
   contact is left after the grace.
 - A fast finger smears into a blob that fails the size / aspect check for a
   frame or two (DisableOnPalm): update() ended the drag and lifted everything.
   A latched drag now rides out 100 ms of invalid frames.
 - An unstable frame (PositionThresholdMax) skipped the singletouch contact,
   which read as a lift: BTN_TOUCH and the tool off for a frame, and BTN_LEFT
   cleared too when no drag was latched yet. The selected contact now stays
   down while it is skipped.
 - The scroll cancel counted any second moving contact, so a transient split of
   a fast blob cancelled the drag. Only contacts that were present for 60 ms
   count as movers now (for the cancel and for starting the latch).
 - At speed the bit can drop before the contact moved DragStartMm. A release
   while the only contact moved 1 mm in the last frame now latches the drag
   instead of releasing the button.

Unstable frames never count as still for DragReleaseStillMs (they restart the
stillness timer, which is the safe direction).

New [Touchpad] DragRelatchMs (default 400, 0 disables): when the dragging
contact lifts with no other contact left (a selection reaching the pad edge),
the button stays held for this long. One new contact landing in that time
continues the same drag without a release or press, and is still subject to the
stillness rule and to lifting again. Two contacts landing (a scroll), or
nothing landing in time, release it. A drag that never moved DragStartMm, and a
plain click, still release at once. The runner timeout covers the window. Trade
off: after a real drag ends by lifting the finger, the drop is delayed by
DragRelatchMs (plus up to LiftGraceMs).

